1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,712
The school expects a diagnostic-quality twelve-lead in under three
minutes from contact in a cooperative, appropriate patient. This is a
specific training target, not permission to omit resuscitation,
consent or correct placement.

2
00:00:14,712 --> 00:00:28,152
Module Seven G also introduces STEMI patterns, equivalents and mimics.
We teach the recognition concepts while keeping current operational
authorization separate from a classroom interpretation exercise.

3
00:00:28,152 --> 00:00:39,648
A lead map suggests anatomical views; it does not prove a culprit
artery or the absence of dangerous disease. The patient and evolving
findings remain central.

4
00:00:39,648 --> 00:00:49,128
The source notes distinguish the saved school wording from current
public BCEHS workflow. Do not combine the two into a new invented
treatment rule.

5
00:00:49,128 --> 00:01:01,008
An electrode is a physical contact. A lead is a calculated view of
electrical activity. Four limb contacts and six chest contacts create
the standard twelve-lead information.

6
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:11,352
The six limb views look in the frontal plane, while the precordial
views sample the horizontal plane. A monitoring strip supplies less
spatial information.

7
00:01:11,352 --> 00:01:21,480
This is not twelve different heartbeats or twelve physical sensors.
The displayed arrangement may vary, so read the actual lead labels and
recording format.

8
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:32,592
Multiple views help recognize regional changes, but correct geometry
and signal quality are necessary. A wrong position can create a
plausible but misleading picture.

9
00:01:32,592 --> 00:01:42,528
Explain that you need skin contacts on the limbs and chest to record
the heart's electrical activity. Describe the exposure and what the
patient will feel.

10
00:01:42,528 --> 00:01:56,496
Obtain appropriate consent and address questions or preferences. An
emergency incapacity situation uses the actual legal process, not a
routine assumption that every chest-pain patient has implicitly
agreed.

11
00:01:56,496 --> 00:02:08,832
Use draping and respectful assistance, particularly when chest
landmarks lie under breast tissue. Do not substitute exposed nipple
position for a true anatomical landmark.

12
00:02:08,832 --> 00:02:20,736
Use a tolerable supine or semi-recumbent position as appropriate and
support limbs. Do not force a breathless patient flat or ask a failing
patient to perform a prolonged breath-hold.

13
00:02:20,736 --> 00:02:33,192
Colour mnemonics depend on the cable standard. Check the printed RA,
LA, RL and LL labels and the actual trained monitor procedure instead
of assuming one colour sequence worldwide.

14
00:02:33,192 --> 00:02:45,264
Use approved limb sites with good contact and avoid placing electrodes
directly over bone. Keep comparable left/right placement and support
the limbs to reduce muscle noise.

15
00:02:45,264 --> 00:02:57,216
Do not casually move diagnostic limb electrodes to the torso just to
make the trace look cleaner. Modified placement can change waveforms
and needs its actual protocol and notation.

16
00:02:57,216 --> 00:03:09,864
If body habitus, injury or access requires deviation, obtain
appropriate guidance and record the actual sites and position so
serial comparisons are not falsely reassuring.

17
00:03:09,864 --> 00:03:20,808
The sternal angle helps identify the second rib. Count intercostal
spaces downward to the fourth space rather than estimating by eye or
by the nipple.

18
00:03:20,808 --> 00:03:29,544
V one is at the right sternal border in the fourth intercostal space,
and V two is at the left sternal border in the fourth space.

19
00:03:29,544 --> 00:03:40,296
Putting V one and V two too high is a common avoidable source of
misleading changes. Confirm the landmark before interpreting an
unexpected anterior pattern.

20
00:03:40,296 --> 00:03:52,176
Practice landmarking on the school model with feedback. A flat diagram
can show relationships but cannot substitute for supervised palpation
on different body shapes.

21
00:03:52,176 --> 00:04:01,968
Find V four at the fifth intercostal space on the mid-clavicular line.
Then place V three between the already located V two and V four.

22
00:04:01,968 --> 00:04:13,512
Place V five at the anterior axillary line and V six at the mid-
axillary line in the same horizontal plane as V four. Do not follow a
descending rib curve around the chest.

23
00:04:13,512 --> 00:04:26,472
Check the contact and actual positions before acquisition. Breast
tissue, adiposity, dressings and clothing should prompt respectful
correct access, not a guess at an approximate spot.

24
00:04:26,472 --> 00:04:35,976
The diagram is relational, not a scale body map. State the landmarks
aloud and ask an instructor to verify placement during practice.

25
00:04:35,976 --> 00:04:48,048
The correct chest-wall location may be under breast tissue. Explain
and provide privacy and appropriate assistance rather than placing a
sensor over an arbitrary more convenient area.

26
00:04:48,048 --> 00:04:57,768
Larger or smaller body size does not replace anatomical landmarking
with a fixed number of centimetres from a nipple. Palpate what you
need to identify.

27
00:04:57,768 --> 00:05:11,184
When injuries, dressings or skin condition prevent usual placement,
use the actual trained alternative and communicate the modification.
Do not interpret a modified trace as if nothing changed.

28
00:05:11,184 --> 00:05:22,680
For repeat ECGs, comparable placement and posture make change more
interpretable. Record important unavoidable differences with the time
and symptoms.

29
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:34,224
Before the encounter, organize the actual equipment and check
consumables. An untangled cable and sound electrodes save time without
cutting patient safeguards.

30
00:05:34,224 --> 00:05:44,688
Prepare the skin using the approved technique, accounting for
moisture, hair and sensitivity. Do not apply an improvised abrasive or
chemical method.

31
00:05:44,688 --> 00:05:55,440
Check connections and relieve traction. When ready, calmly ask the
patient to rest the limbs, avoid talking and breathe normally for the
short acquisition.

32
00:05:55,440 --> 00:06:07,752
When acquisition fails, troubleshoot a specific cause instead of
repeatedly pressing print. Continue assessment and avoid turning an
equipment problem into a prolonged scene delay.

33
00:06:07,752 --> 00:06:17,520
Verify that all twelve views are present and labelled, with readable
complexes and a usable baseline. Check speed and gain before
measuring.

34
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:27,336
Enter accurate identity and device-required demographic data. Wrong
patient details can misdirect a transmission or influence automated
processing.

35
00:06:27,336 --> 00:06:39,696
Record when symptoms and acquisition occurred and what care was given.
Select the correct ECG record for transmission; sending a previous
person's tracing is not a minor labelling issue.

36
00:06:39,696 --> 00:06:50,040
Check the actual transmission result and communicate failure through
the appropriate alternative. A send button alone does not prove the
receiving team has the tracing.

37
00:06:50,040 --> 00:07:02,952
Start the practice clock at patient contact, as the school specifies
for a cooperative appropriate patient. Assign a primary assessor and
an acquisition partner so necessary tasks run in parallel.

38
00:07:02,952 --> 00:07:16,872
Use the first practice to locate the bottleneck: equipment
preparation, exposure/consent, landmarking, data entry or
troubleshooting. Improve that step rather than sacrificing accuracy.

39
00:07:16,872 --> 00:07:27,984
Stop the clock only when the required tracing is actually acquired and
readable, not when the last electrode is attached. Record actual time
and any quality failure.

40
00:07:27,984 --> 00:07:40,080
If urgent breathing or circulation support takes priority, state that
and provide it. Do not meet a classroom target by delaying a more
important intervention or falsely documenting a time.

41
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:51,888
The current handbook has separate LifePak Fifteen and ZOLL acquisition
pages. Buttons, demographics, noise handling and transmission workflow
are not interchangeable.

42
00:07:51,888 --> 00:08:02,952
The school exercise must be practised on its actual monitor. This
lecture supplies the reasoning and sequence, not a claim that a
generic diagram trains every device.

43
00:08:02,952 --> 00:08:11,688
Do not invent an override when noisy data prevents a recording. Follow
the device-specific authorization and obtain appropriate help.

44
00:08:11,688 --> 00:08:24,168
The current PCP workflow warns to leave the twelve-lead acquisition
screen when not actively acquiring because ongoing rhythm detection
can be affected. Confirm the actual monitoring mode after recording.

45
00:08:24,168 --> 00:08:33,672
An early ECG can be nondiagnostic while disease evolves. Save the
initial record and link it to the symptom state and relevant
treatment.

46
00:08:33,672 --> 00:08:44,256
Repeat according to the actual pathway and clinical change.
Deterioration requires immediate reassessment, not waiting for a
scheduled ten-minute recording.

47
00:08:44,256 --> 00:08:55,488
Compare leads, position, speed and gain as well as the waveform. A
changed electrode position can create an apparent trend that is not a
physiological change.

48
00:08:55,488 --> 00:09:05,592
Report the evolving pattern with the patient response and time. A
repeat that looks less dramatic does not independently establish that
the danger has resolved.

49
00:09:05,592 --> 00:09:19,656
The school uses inferior leads Two, Three and aVF; septal views V one
and V two; anterior views V three and V four; and lateral views One,
aVL, V five and V six.

50
00:09:19,656 --> 00:09:32,088
These are useful teaching groupings. Anterior and septal regions
overlap in clinical interpretation, and the standard lead label does
not isolate a perfectly bounded piece of myocardium.

51
00:09:32,088 --> 00:09:42,576
Contiguous means anatomically related views. Two traces beside each
other in the printed layout are not automatically a qualifying
anatomical pair.

52
00:09:42,576 --> 00:09:53,736
Inspect all leads rather than only the region you expect from the
symptom story. A prior infarction or a familiar machine phrase can
bias attention away from a new pattern.

53
00:09:53,736 --> 00:10:05,952
The common teaching associations are inferior with right coronary
supply, anterior/septal with the left anterior descending artery and
lateral with circumflex-related supply.

54
00:10:05,952 --> 00:10:18,312
Coronary dominance, branching and overlap vary. Inferior changes are
not proof that the right coronary artery is the culprit, and lateral
supply is not identical in every person.

55
00:10:18,312 --> 00:10:30,624
Localization helps anticipate complications such as conduction
disturbance, pump failure or right ventricular involvement, but it is
a risk-oriented inference, not definitive anatomy.

56
00:10:30,624 --> 00:10:40,752
Communicate the exact leads and clinical state. That is more
defensible than declaring a complete vessel occlusion or a specific
lesion from a teaching map alone.

57
00:10:40,752 --> 00:10:51,408
Locate QRS end before describing the ST segment. The J point is the
boundary between the end of ventricular depolarization and the
following segment.

58
00:10:51,408 --> 00:11:03,840
Use a reliable reference and the actual calibration. Baseline wander,
a wide QRS, pacing or other repolarization changes can make a simple
displacement measurement misleading.

59
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:16,176
The original figure illustrates a baseline segment, elevation and
depression at the same represented scale. A single illustrated change
is not a full STEMI diagnostic example.

60
00:11:16,176 --> 00:11:26,400
Describe what you measured and where. Do not equate every raised
segment with infarction or every inverted T with completed
irreversible damage.

61
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:40,104
For the usual adult reference criteria, new J-point ST elevation is
assessed in at least two contiguous leads. The general threshold is
one millimetre, with a specific exception for V two and V three.

62
00:11:40,104 --> 00:11:50,232
In V two and V three the cited thresholds are two point five
millimetres for men under forty, two for men forty or older and one
point five for women.

63
00:11:50,232 --> 00:12:04,488
These are qualified reference criteria, not a rule to apply
indiscriminately with bundle-branch block, ventricular pacing or
marked hypertrophy. Other ischemic patterns may be dangerous without
satisfying this table.

64
00:12:04,488 --> 00:12:18,480
Learn the measurements for the school interpretation task, but use the
actual PCP acquisition, flagging and consultation pathway
operationally. Manual recognition does not create independent
reperfusion authority.

65
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:32,136
In original example A, One point five millimetres in Two and aVF
exceeds the general one-millimetre reference in an inferior pair,
assuming valid acquisition and the required clinical conditions.

66
00:12:32,136 --> 00:12:45,888
Example B exceeds the cited one point five millimetre V two/V three
threshold for women. Example C does not meet the two point five
millimetre exception for men under forty.

67
00:12:45,888 --> 00:12:56,904
Not meeting that numeric table is not a safe rule-out. Symptoms, other
leads, reciprocal findings, evolution, equivalents and confounding
conduction patterns still matter.

68
00:12:56,904 --> 00:13:08,712
None of these isolated numbers proves the artery, extent or current
viability of myocardium. Describe concern and follow the appropriate
clinical review and workflow.

69
00:13:08,712 --> 00:13:21,168
The school describes ischemia, injury and infarction with T-wave, ST
and Q-wave changes. Use this to understand possible evolution, not as
a mandatory timeline.

70
00:13:21,168 --> 00:13:32,664
An acute event may lack a textbook sequence; Q waves can be old,
absent or affected by other factors. Waiting for Q waves can delay
time-sensitive care.

71
00:13:32,664 --> 00:13:44,472
A Q wave does not alone confirm that all affected tissue is
irreversibly dead, and ST elevation does not prove full-thickness
necrosis or complete occlusion in every case.

72
00:13:44,472 --> 00:13:56,400
Integrate symptoms, serial ECGs, specialist review and hospital
testing. In the field, recognize concern early rather than waiting for
a complete diagnostic story.

73
00:13:56,400 --> 00:14:05,520
A posterior coronary event may show ST depression in anterior views
rather than the obvious standard-lead elevation a beginner expects.

74
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:17,016
The school highlights depression and tall R waves in V one to V three
as clues. They raise concern in context; they do not diagnose the
cause of every anterior depression.

75
00:14:17,016 --> 00:14:31,752
Additional posterior views may help under the actual authorized plan.
The current ZOLL procedure labels posterior and right-sided
acquisition ACP-only, so this classroom concept does not create an
independent PCP procedure.

76
00:14:31,752 --> 00:14:42,480
If electrodes are moved by an authorized clinician, the new
designations must be clear. A V seven tracing labelled V four can
mislead the receiving team.

77
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:52,608
Left bundle-branch block changes ventricular activation and secondary
ST-T appearance. This makes ordinary elevation measurements harder to
interpret.

78
00:14:52,608 --> 00:15:04,704
The saved school wording calls a new or presumed-new LBBB a STEMI
equivalent in context. Current ACS guidance says it is not diagnostic
of acute infarction in isolation.

79
00:15:04,704 --> 00:15:19,008
Symptoms, hemodynamic state, comparison with prior tracings and
specialist criteria such as Sgarbossa can change the concern. This is
not permission for a beginner to rule in or rule out by one wide
complex.

80
00:15:19,008 --> 00:15:31,296
Treat instability, obtain appropriate support and communicate the
exact findings and uncertainty. A normal automated phrase or uncertain
onset does not settle the clinical problem.

81
00:15:31,296 --> 00:15:43,176
Noncoronary and nonacute patterns can produce ST changes, including
early repolarization, pericarditis, ventricular hypertrophy and
abnormal ventricular activation.

82
00:15:43,176 --> 00:15:52,488
Technical error can also mimic disease. Confirm correct placement and
signal, especially when a pattern conflicts with the clinical picture.

83
00:15:52,488 --> 00:16:03,912
Do not rule out coronary disease simply by recognizing one feature
associated with a mimic. A person can have a baseline abnormality and
a new event together.

84
00:16:03,912 --> 00:16:12,072
Communicate the concern and seek appropriate review rather than
creating a confident benign diagnosis from a short classroom
checklist.

85
00:16:12,072 --> 00:16:25,488
A normal or nondiagnostic ECG does not exclude acute coronary
syndrome. NSTEMI can have nonspecific or absent ECG changes and
requires the broader clinical and hospital assessment.

86
00:16:25,488 --> 00:16:37,920
Dangerous pulmonary, vascular and other conditions can also occur with
a normal tracing. Do not cancel supportive care, transport or
escalation solely because the machine says normal.

87
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:49,752
A person without chest pain can still have an acute coronary
presentation. Consider the actual symptom pattern, including
breathlessness, weakness, nausea or syncope.

88
00:16:49,752 --> 00:17:00,264
Repeat assessment and obtain indicated review. Improvement of one
symptom or one less dramatic tracing does not independently prove
safety.

89
00:17:00,264 --> 00:17:12,768
The school requires verbalizing what else could this be before the
first intervention. The purpose is to prevent anchoring, not to demand
that every alternative be definitively excluded before help begins.

90
00:17:12,768 --> 00:17:24,648
For chest pressure, important alternatives may include aortic disease,
pulmonary embolism or pneumothorax as well as other causes. State a
relevant cue that would change the plan.

91
00:17:24,648 --> 00:17:36,984
A focused cognitive check can run alongside oxygenation, ventilation
and circulation support. It must never become a reason to delay CPR or
other immediately indicated care.

92
00:17:36,984 --> 00:17:48,432
After each action, ask what changed, what should have changed and
whether anything worsened. A new finding should reopen the working
explanation instead of being ignored.

93
00:17:48,432 --> 00:17:58,632
Recognizing a concerning trace is not the end of assessment. Continue
support and confirm whether the planned medicine or procedure is
appropriate for this patient now.

94
00:17:58,632 --> 00:18:15,024
Use actual local indications and safeguards for antiplatelet and
vasodilator treatment. Hypotension, possible right ventricular
involvement and interacting medicines can matter; an ECG label alone
is not a nitroglycerin instruction.

95
00:18:15,024 --> 00:18:27,768
Oxygen follows the applicable clinical target and exceptions, not a
routine response to every chest-pain call. Do not silently replace the
current A seven wording with a school blanket range.

96
00:18:27,768 --> 00:18:37,920
Plan rapid appropriate conveyance and early communication. An IV
attempt or a desire for a prettier printout must not create an
avoidable delay.

97
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:50,880
The current public PCP workflow uses a STEMI flag to direct the local
pathway, transmission and notification. Request the appropriate
support without delaying on scene simply to wait for an intercept.

98
00:18:50,880 --> 00:19:06,576
An ACUTE MI flag without STEMI does not automatically activate that
PCP pathway. The workflow calls for clinical consultation, appropriate
support and repeat twelve-leads every ten minutes, with immediate
reassessment for deterioration.

99
00:19:06,576 --> 00:19:17,832
These are operational distinctions, not a claim that automated
software proves or excludes infarction. Follow your actual monitor
training and applicable local requirements.

100
00:19:17,832 --> 00:19:28,992
Return to the proper monitoring screen after acquisition and document
the chosen route of communication. Do not invent a universal
destination from a regional pathway used elsewhere.

101
00:19:28,992 --> 00:19:38,736
Use the appropriate handover or consultation structure. State your
role, the patient's presentation and the time of symptom onset and
acquisition.

102
00:19:38,736 --> 00:19:51,312
Describe exact lead findings, the machine flag and any signal
limitation. Separate observation from inference, such as inferior
elevation rather than certainty about a particular artery.

103
00:19:51,312 --> 00:20:03,864
Include blood pressure and pulse trends, awareness, symptoms,
important alternatives and the response to care. Say if a repeat has
changed and whether the tracing was actually transmitted.

104
00:20:03,864 --> 00:20:16,368
Finish with the intended destination or advice request, estimated
arrival and closed-loop confirmation. A clear uncertainty statement is
safer than an unsupported confident label.

105
00:20:16,368 --> 00:20:26,208
This is an original teaching case, not the school Wednesday patient. A
fifty-eight-year-old has new pressure and nausea with a history of
infarction.

106
00:20:26,208 --> 00:20:36,936
Obtain appropriate primary assessment and a quality tracing, while the
team considers alternatives. Prior infarction does not prove every
current change is old.

107
00:20:36,936 --> 00:20:48,936
The new inferior pattern and falling pressure demand attention to
perfusion and possible complications. Do not give a vasodilator
automatically because the complaint is chest pain.

108
00:20:48,936 --> 00:21:03,864
Follow the actual flag/local pathway, obtain support, communicate the
lead findings and hypotension, and reassess after care. Do not delay
conveyance to finish a perfect culprit-artery explanation.

109
00:21:03,864 --> 00:21:15,840
The saved Seven G lesson asks learners to interpret a series of
twelve-lead strips and submit findings. The actual assessed strip set
and current submission settings are not recovered here.

110
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:27,216
Use an original practice sheet to organize your own observations:
quality and settings, rhythm features, regional changes, clinical
context and appropriate next steps.

111
00:21:27,216 --> 00:21:38,784
Distinguish what is visible from what cannot be known without
symptoms, prior traces or further assessment. Do not present a
synthetic example as the answer to a missing assessed strip.

112
00:21:38,784 --> 00:21:47,592
This course can teach and provide practice; it does not submit work,
open a quiz or certify the school assignment as completed.

113
00:21:47,592 --> 00:21:57,528
Point to each anatomical landmark on the training model and explain
why V four precedes V three and why V five and V six stay level.

114
00:21:57,528 --> 00:22:06,024
Rehearse the under-three-minute school target with a partner,
measuring actual quality and time rather than treating speed alone as
success.

115
00:22:06,024 --> 00:22:16,584
Explain the V two/V three threshold exception, why new LBBB is not an
isolated diagnosis and why a normal tracing cannot rule out ACS.

116
00:22:16,584 --> 00:22:26,400
Finish with a concise report and the school reassessment questions.
The important endpoint is a safer care sequence, not a memorized
vessel label.
